 "observations": [
  "The page number «144» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 33», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03, the place of Jupiter's node, in smaller bold type, «Sig. 2, Gr. 22, Dak. 1», «Dak.» for the minutes, the Arabic daqāʾiq that C and O often use, Latin «dakaica» (Suter, p. 36, JP2 0068), followed by «(OC)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the last title in two lines, «C 59r, O 116v,» and «N 29v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 143 (JP2 0175), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Semitae numerorum (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium1)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium1)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio ascensus Jovis examinata2)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Jovis»; «Latitudo Jovis accedentis» over «prima4)» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L08), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L09, the units, in bold.",
  "L03: the node of Jupiter, two signs 22° 1′, is the value of chapter 19 of the text (p. 15, JP2 0047) and of p. 64 (JP2 0096, L38), 82° 1′.",
  "L10-L39. Checked: each value of the Sublimatio is 5s 22° 32′, the apogee of Jupiter (text, chapter 18, p. 14, JP2 0046; 172° 32′), less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 6°, where 5s 22° 3′ stands for 5s 22° 3½′, and at 27°, where 5s 20° 27′ stands for 5s 20° 26′. The Examinatio argumenti agrees to within 0.6′ in every row with the equation of the anomaly of an epicycle, arc tan (r sin x : (60 + r cos x)) as for Saturn (p. 138, JP2 0170), for r = 11.35, the radius that fits the thirty values best.",
  "L10-L39. The Latitudo secunda is 2° 30′ · sin x to within 0.5″ in every row except four; its greatest value, 2° 30′, is to be compared with p. 146. At 3°, 0° 7′ 11″ is 40″ less than 0° 7′ 51.0″, and at 24°, 1° 1′ 41″ is 40.4″ more than 1° 1′ 0.6″: the steps from 2° to 4°, 1′ 57″ and 3′ 17″, and from 23° to 25°, 3′ 4″ and 1′ 43″, stand against about 2′ 37″ and 2′ 23″. At 18°, 0° 46′ 28″ is 6.8″ more than 0° 46′ 21.2″, and at 28°, 1° 10′ 29″ is 3.8″ more than 1° 10′ 25.2″. No note of the apparatus bears on these four figures.",
  "L10-L39, the other columns, compared from row to row: the Examinatio centri increases by 4′ to 6′ a degree, to 2° 33′ at 30°, the half of 5° 6′; it lies within 1.7′ below 5° 6′ · sin x in every row. The Statio prima increases from 4s 4° 5′ to 4s 4° 17′: by 1′ every third degree to 23°, then by 1′ a degree. The Latitudo prima decreases from 1° 36′ at 1° by 1′ every fourth degree, to 1° 28′ at 29° and 30°.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 1s 0°, is N's; C and O have 0. Notes b-f: C leaves out the sign of the Sublimatio from 3° to 14° and from 17° to 28°, and its degrees, 22, 21 and 20, from 2° to 5°, from 8° to 18° and from 21° to 29°; each range is marked at its first and its last row. Checked: the editor's readings agree with the rules above where the variants depart: the Sublimatio 5s 21° 16′ at 16° (note g, against C's 13); the Examinatio centri 2° 23′ at 28° (note h); the degree 1 of the Latitudo secunda from 24° to 30° (notes k-k, against C's 0); its 1° 3′ 24″ at 25° (note l, C), 1° 3′ 23.6″ computed. At 9° (note i) the edition prints 4s 4° 6′ against the 5 of O and N and of C, corrected there from 6; the column is otherwise steady, and 5 would fall back below the 6 of 7° and 8°. The note does not say «correxi».",
  "Italics: «om.» and «corr. ex». N01: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. N03-N05: the ranges are printed «b)-b)», each letter raised with its parenthesis, where pp. 139 and 142 (JP2 0171, 0174) print «c-c)» and «f-f)»."
 ],
